    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d647">

                <pb facs="1329" n="1317" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg1317"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">General Marshall to Mr. Walter S. Robertson, at
                        Peiping</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-07-08">8 July 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1068. Correspondence has reached this office from both sides in the past few days
                    concerning the Hankow Communist situation. Each side accuses the other of
                    offensive action and therefore each side under the guise of self-defense seems
                    to be engaging in pitted warfare. Would you please give me urgently a current
                    report on this situation together with your recommendation. In his last
                        memo<anchor n="79" xml:id="d647-f1"/> on the subject General Chou has
                    brought up again the idea of moving the Communist forces northward, thus
                    relieving cause for continued friction. I doubt however that the Government
                    would approve such a measure at this time.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
